my name is emily riehl i'm a  mathematician at johns hopkins  university in baltimore  and i work on infinite dimensional  category theory  bill thurston has this really lovely  essay on proof and progress in  mathematics  and he points out that progress in  mathematics is made not just through  proving new theorems  but also through advancing human  understanding of mathematics  so there are some theorems that i've  proven that i'm very proud of but i'm  also proud of the work that i've done on  expository writing  i've written some textbooks trying to  communicate the mathematics that i love  to a broader audience than i'd be able  to in a research paper alone  so in an orchestra there are instruments  that sound spectacular  individually and they're often given the  soloist roles  what the viola contributes is it really  adds a layer of depth  to the sound it's tying together the  high pitches and the violins and the  low tones and the bases  you know frankly i think uh the pieces  sound like garbage if you don't have the  viola  in there and similarly category theory  is adding depth by recognizing  connections between theorems that are  proven in abstract algebra and  theorems that are proven in points at  topology category theory recognizes that  a mathematical argument that's  discussing quite different objects  can be sort of fundamentally the same  [Music]  i think it's important for  mathematicians to recognize that  not all of our students have exactly the  same background  some of us fell in love with mathematics  at a very  young age and others they'll discover  their  passion for mathematics much later in  life some people are very comfortable  using the lingo that you pick up if  you're hanging out with the  kids in the math common room whereas  others never felt quite as welcome in  that space and don't speak quite the  same language  so one of the things that i think about  a lot as an educator  is whether the language i'm using would  alienate the students in my classroom  who haven't understood  everything that i've said quite yet and  we're really  very narrow-minded if we think that  everything that we say is  absorbed immediately by whoever is  listening
 

